LPHABET_TOO_SHORT`, `NANOID_ALPHABET_TOO_LONG`"},"442":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-52","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#update-error-code-matches","content":"`ALPHABET_OUT_OF_RANGE`"},"443":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-53","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#update-error-code-matches","content":"`NANOID_ALPHABET_INVALID_CHAR`"},"444":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-54","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#update-error-code-matches","content":"`ALPHABET_INVALID_CHAR`"},"445":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-55","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#update-error-code-matches","content":"`NANOID_ALPHABET_DUPLICATE`"},"446":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-56","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#update-error-code-matches","content":"`ALPHABET_DUPLICATE`"},"447":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-57","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#update-error-code-matches","content":"`NANOID_SIZE_INVALID`, `NANOID_SIZE_TOO_LARGE`, `CUID2_LENGTH_OUT_OF_RANGE`"},"448":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-58","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#update-error-code-matches","content":"`LENGTH_OUT_OF_RANGE`"},"449":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-59","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#update-error-code-matches","content":"Several old codes intentionally converge on one v1 meaning. This only changes error handling; generated and persisted identifier data is unaffected."},"450":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-60","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#removed-codes","content":"`ULID_RANDOM_OVERFLOW` has no v1 replacement. Exhausting ULID's 80-bit monotonic\nrandom portion no longer fails: the generator advances its timestamp by one\nmillisecond and continues, which is what UUID v7 already did with its counter. The\n`RANDOM_OVERFLOW` code that briefly replaced it is gone for the same reason — there\nis no longer a failure to report. If you matched on either code, delete the branch."},"451":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-61","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#removed-codes","content":"A ULID or UUID v7 whose 48-bit timestamp field is already at its maximum now\nthrows `TIMESTAMP_OUT_OF_RANGE` instead, because borrowing the next millisecond\nwould no longer fit."},"452":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-62","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#what-remains-stable","content":"Within `1.x`, uniku will not remove or rename documented entry points, exports, options, methods, or constants. Canonical string formats, byte order, timestamp units, and documented error codes are part of the contract."},"453":{"id":"/docs/migration/v1-63","page_id":"/docs/migration/v1","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/migration/v1#what-remains-stable","content":"Read the stability contract for the full release and runtime policy."},"454":{"id":"/docs/reference/cuid-v2","page_id":"/docs/reference/cuid-v2","type":"page","content":"CUID v2","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/cuid-v2"},"455":{"id":"/docs/reference/cuid-v2-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/cuid-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/cuid-v2","content":"Non-sequential, string-native identifiers that resist enumeration."},"456":{"id":"/docs/reference/cuid-v2-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/cuid-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/cuid-v2","content":"CUID v2 is for public values where predictability is the problem. It is string-native, so there is no byte codec to maintain or expose."},"457":{"id":"/docs/reference/entry-points","page_id":"/docs/reference/entry-points","type":"page","content":"Entry points","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/entry-points"},"458":{"id":"/docs/reference/entry-points-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/entry-points","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/entry-points","content":"Eleven stable generator entry points behind one consistent, type-safe API."},"459":{"id":"/docs/reference/entry-points-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/entry-points","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/entry-points#how-modules-differ","content":"How modules differ"},"460":{"id":"/docs/reference/entry-points-2","page_id":"/docs/reference/entry-points","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/entry-points#generator-references","content":"Generator references"},"461":{"id":"/docs/reference/entry-points-3","page_id":"/docs/reference/entry-points","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/entry-points","content":"`uniku` exposes eleven ID generation modules. Each module exports a callable generator with attached helpers. The most common public API looks like this:"},"462":{"id":"/docs/reference/entry-points-4","page_id":"/docs/reference/entry-points","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/entry-points","content":"Binary modules can also write directly into a caller-owned buffer:"},"463":{"id":"/docs/reference/entry-points-5","page_id":"/docs/reference/entry-points","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/entry-points#how-modules-differ","content":"This table is derived from the generated TypeScript API reference. Open a module's page for complete signatures, options, constants, and examples."},"464":{"id":"/docs/reference/entry-points-6","page_id":"/docs/reference/entry-points","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/entry-points#how-modules-differ","content":"`uuid/v5` is the one name-based module: it derives an ID from a name and a namespace instead of generating a new one, so it takes positional `(name, namespace, buf?, offset?)` arguments rather than an options object, keeps no state, and has no `timestamp()`."},"465":{"id":"/do